# Copy to `.env.local` for development. Every variable here is `NEXT_PUBLIC_`,
# which means Next.js inlines it into the browser bundle at build time: it is
# readable by anyone using the site, so no secret belongs in this file. Values
# for the deployed environments are set in the Vercel project, not here.

# Where the Django API lives. Production: https://api.weight.jgdx.xyz
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000

# Shown to drivers under Settings and attached to field-staff diagnostics.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_VERSION=0.1.0

# --- Map tiles -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by `src/lib/map-tiles.ts`, which every map on the platform draws through.
#
# Leaving these unset falls back to OpenStreetMap's public tile servers. That is
# fine locally and NOT fine in production: the OSM Tile Usage Policy does not
# cover a commercial product serving tiles to every contractor, recycler and
# driver, and enforcement is a block at their end that would blank every map on
# the platform at once.
#
# Before serving real customers, set these to a provider that sells the right to
# do this (MapTiler, Mapbox, Stadia and others all offer a free tier well above
# a two-company pilot). The key usually travels inside the URL.
#
# Example (MapTiler):
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL=https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets-v2/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION=&copy; MapTiler &copy; OpenStreetMap contributors
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_MAX_ZOOM=22
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION=
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_MAX_ZOOM=
